ed it there.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"All BQN solutions outperformed everything else in this [perf wars](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7n262KKvHj8) video, this file explains the optimizations BQN has which led it there.","submitter_user":"veqq","user_is_author":false,"tags":["apl","performance"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/lqelb1","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/lqelb1/why_bqn_wins"},{"short_id":"wlofzl","created_at":"2026-08-17T19:47:13.022-05:00","title":"The benchmarkpocalypse","url":"https://danluu.com/benchpocalypse/","score":14,"flags":0,"comment_count":2,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"nolan","user_is_author":false,"tags":["performance","vibecoding"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/wlofzl","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/wlofzl/benchmarkpocalypse"},{"short_id":"j5xjf2","created_at":"2026-08-17T17:58:03.027-05:00","title":"Quake Shareware, a CD-ROM just a little too full","url":"https://fabiensanglard.net/quake_shareware_cd/index.html","score":22,"flags":0,"comment_count":3,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"classichasclass","user_is_author":false,"tags":["games","historical","reversing"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/j5xjf2","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/j5xjf2/quake_shareware_cd_rom_just_little_too"},{"short_id":"izkimy","created_at":"2026-08-17T17:42:21.702-05:00","title":"Retrofitting a build system into a compiler","url":"https://www.dra27.uk/blog/platform/2025/09/25/building-with-effects.html","score":7,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"tmcgilchrist","user_is_author":false,"tags":["compilers","ml"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/izkimy","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/izkimy/retrofitting_build_system_into_compiler"},{"short_id":"dan3jj","created_at":"2026-08-17T17:08:22.682-05:00","title":"A Dual View on Syntax","url":"https://text.marvinborner.de/2026-08-11-17.html","score":2,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eInteraction nets are closely related to optimal reduction, and thus are a candidate for the basis of an interesting functional programming language compiler/runtime, like HVM.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEven if you don't define your language semantics with optimal reduction, using flat, relational data instead of recursive data for your internal representation is a great fit for query-based architecture (which is just logic/database programming) and can simplify a lot of dynamic programming problems that come up in language implementation. - \u003ca href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/ProgrammingLanguages/comments/1vqq6z6/a_dual_view_on_syntax/p485hkm/\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003einitial-algebra\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n","description_plain":"\u003e Interaction nets are closely related to optimal reduction, and thus are a candidate for the basis of an interesting functional programming language compiler/runtime, like HVM.\r\n\u003e \r\n\u003e Even if you don't define your language semantics with optimal reduction, using flat, relational data instead of recursive data for your internal representation is a great fit for query-based architecture (which is just logic/database programming) and can simplify a lot of dynamic programming problems that come up in language implementation. - [initial-algebra](https://www.reddit.com/r/ProgrammingLanguages/comments/1vqq6z6/a_dual_view_on_syntax/p485hkm/)","submitter_user":"veqq","user_is_author":false,"tags":["plt"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/dan3jj","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/dan3jj/dual_view_on_syntax"},{"short_id":"g74nil","created_at":"2026-08-17T16:50:49.180-05:00","title":"Self Extracting Tar Files","url":"https://www.vinnie.work/blog/2024-05-24-self-extracting-tarfiles","score":2,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"dv","user_is_author":false,"tags":["programming"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/g74nil","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/g74nil/self_extracting_tar_files"},{"short_id":"05ocit","created_at":"2026-08-17T16:46:01.672-05:00","title":"How Bluesky draws its logo on screenshots","url":"https://timmarinin.net/2026/bluesky-screenshots/","score":62,"flags":0,"comment_count":6,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"mt","user_is_author":true,"tags":["ios"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/05ocit","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/05ocit/how_bluesky_draws_its_logo_on_screenshots"},{"short_id":"bx8zu1","created_at":"2026-08-17T16:45:53.106-05:00","title":"A practical workflow for LLM-assisted development","url":"https://yogthos.net/posts/2026-08-17-llm-workflow.html","score":6,"flags":1,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"Yogthos","user_is_author":true,"tags":["vibecoding"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/bx8zu1","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/bx8zu1/practical_workflow_for_llm_assisted"},{"short_id":"u8nf3e","created_at":"2026-08-17T15:53:35.901-05:00","title":"An Open Protocol for Knowledge/Memory Management","url":"","score":-4,"flags":5,"comment_count":0,"description":"\u003cp\u003eRecently, I got to thinking about what it might look like to open-source the core fundamentals of my AllSign project. As I fell further and further down that conceptual rabbit hole, it occurred to me that those core fundamentals are actually a really good solution to a problem I saw with agent memory.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI read this paper called \"Filesystem-Based Memory for LLM Agents\", \u003ca href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.26637\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ehttps://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.26637\u003c/a\u003e, which concluded (rightfully so) that \"information gathering and organization does not automatically get you intelligence\". I agree.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI thought to myself, what's missing is \"curation\". Something has to decide what's worth keeping, what replaces what, what's true, and what quietly stopped being true.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eKnowledge/memory management that supports HITL and HOTL.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThen I thought, what if those AllSign core fundamentals could be turned into an open protocol for actor-agnostic knowledge management?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThat led me to create the Facts protocol and Fact CLI.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIf you're curious, you can read more here: \u003ca href=\"https://gist.github.com/iamalnewkirk/22ae2d8c8cc1b8790236da9edf2f7b73\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ehttps://gist.github.com/iamalnewkirk/22ae2d8c8cc1b8790236da9edf2f7b73\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/facts-kms/cli\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ehttps://github.com/facts-kms/cli\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/facts-kms/spec\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ehttps://github.com/facts-kms/spec\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n","description_plain":"Recently, I got to thinking about what it might look like to open-source the core fundamentals of my AllSign project. As I fell further and further down that conceptual rabbit hole, it occurred to me that those core fundamentals are actually a really good solution to a problem I saw with agent memory.\r\n\r\nI read this paper called \"Filesystem-Based Memory for LLM Agents\", https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.26637, which concluded (rightfully so) that \"information gathering and organization does not automatically get you intelligence\". I agree.\r\n\r\nI thought to myself, what's missing is \"curation\". Something has to decide what's worth keeping, what replaces what, what's true, and what quietly stopped being true.\r\n\r\nKnowledge/memory management that supports HITL and HOTL.\r\n\r\nThen I thought, what if those AllSign core fundamentals could be turned into an open protocol for actor-agnostic knowledge management?\r\n\r\nThat led me to create the Facts protocol and Fact CLI.\r\n\r\nIf you're curious, you can read more here: https://gist.github.com/iamalnewkirk/22ae2d8c8cc1b8790236da9edf2f7b73\r\n\r\n- https://github.com/facts-kms/cli\r\n- https://github.com/facts-kms/spec","submitter_user":"iamalnewkirk","user_is_author":true,"tags":["programming"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/u8nf3e","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/u8nf3e/open_protocol_for_knowledge_memory"},{"short_id":"sfthhb","created_at":"2026-08-17T15:48:04.211-05:00","title":"Ledger CLI: Plain Text Accounting","url":"https://elnurbda.com/posts/62-ledger-cli/","score":2,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"makishimu","user_is_author":false,"tags":["finance"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/sfthhb","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/sfthhb/ledger_cli_plain_text_accounting"},{"short_id":"fx9pic","created_at":"2026-08-17T15:14:47.873-05:00","title":"AI;DR (AI; Didn’t Read)","url":"https://www.rickmanelius.com/p/aidr-ai-didnt-read","score":18,"flags":5,"comment_count":8,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"dhruvp","user_is_author":false,"tags":["vibecoding"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/fx9pic","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/fx9pic/ai_dr_ai_didn_t_read"},{"short_id":"ttrnw5","created_at":"2026-08-17T13:43:40.171-05:00","title":"How Go detects struct copies with sync.noCopy","url":"https://func25.dev/posts/go-sync-nocopy/","score":1,"flags":0,"comment_count":5,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"rrm","user_is_author":false,"tags":["go"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ttrnw5","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ttrnw5/how_go_detects_struct_copies_with_sync"},{"short_id":"flcpeu","created_at":"2026-08-17T12:54:19.965-05:00","title":"We Tracked a Shipment of Rare Books. It Ended at an Amazon AI Training Facility","url":"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/17/we-tracked-a-shipment-of-rare-books-it-ended-at-an-amazon-ai-tra/","score":15,"flags":3,"comment_count":10,"descripti